title: "2. The value premium in the data"
aliases: [/financial-data.html]
---

[Chapter 1](01-two-free-numbers.qmd) argued that a DCF's two free numbers cannot price cash-flow risk. Before building the model that can, we need the facts the model must match. The chapter establishes them from the 1930 to 2003 sample the paper uses. Value stocks out-earned growth stocks by about six percentage points a year while trading at persistently lower valuations, so the higher return and the lower price go together. The obvious repair fails in the data as it failed in the model, because value's market beta is nowhere near large enough to explain six points.

One rule governs the construction. The series we construct (returns, dividends, price-dividend ratios, and consumption) are targets for the model rather than inputs to it. Average returns enter none of the model's inputs, and they appear only in the tables the model has to match. A model whose target facts leaked into its inputs would prove nothing.

We sort stocks into portfolios by book-to-market equity, which is book value from the accounting statements divided by market value from prices, following Fama and French (1993). The sort forms June quintiles of ordinary shares with NYSE breakpoints. Growth is the bottom fifth, which is expensive relative to book value. Value is the top fifth, which is cheap relative to book value, and the market is the value-weighted whole. The sort hands the model two claims whose dividends behave differently, so any difference in prices and premia must come from cash-flow risk rather than from an assumption.

## The economics: recovering cash flows from returns

The inconvenient fact of the construction is that CRSP, the standard file of U.S. stock returns, stores returns rather than dividends. It records two versions of each month's return: $r_t$, which includes the dividend, and $r_t^x$, the capital gain alone. Campbell and Shiller (1988) noticed that the gap between them is the dividend, and recovered it by scaling with a cumulated price index $V$:

$$
D_t = \left(r_t - r_t^{x}\right) V_{t-1},
\qquad
V_t = V_{t-1}\left(1 + r_t^{x}\right).
$$ {#eq-cs}

In words, whatever the with-dividend return earned beyond the price return was the dividend, and the dividend was paid on last month's value.

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Derivation: why the identity recovers dividends"}
For a portfolio worth $V_{t-1}$ at the end of last month, the two return definitions are

$$
1 + r_t = \frac{V_t + D_t}{V_{t-1}}, \qquad 1 + r_t^{x} = \frac{V_t}{V_{t-1}},
$$

where $V_t$ is this month's ex-dividend value. Subtracting the second from the first gives $r_t - r_t^{x} = D_t / V_{t-1}$, which is @eq-cs. Cumulating $V$ with the capital-gain return alone keeps the index self-consistent, because dividends are paid out rather than reinvested into the price index. Summing the monthly $D_t$ within a calendar year gives the annual dividend; the year-end $V$ over that sum is the price-dividend ratio.
:::

Dividends and prices from @eq-cs are nominal. We deflate them by the PCE price deflator, because the household in the model consumes real goods. Log differences of real annual dividends give dividend growth $\Delta d$. Two more series complete the dataset. Consumption is real per-capita nondurables plus services from the national accounts, because durables behave like investment rather than consumption. The risk-free rate is the real return on the three-month Treasury bill, computed as the nominal bill return less a twelve-month moving average of inflation. One month of inflation is too noisy to be anyone's forecast.

The full pipeline, which pulls CRSP through WRDS, Compustat book equity, and the Davis-Fama-French book values for the years before 1960, is described in the appendix at the end of the chapter. The main argument does not need it. The repository ships the three files the pipeline writes, and every number below is computed live from them.

