Read the middle columns before the first one. Value's dividend growth is more volatile, at 47.72 percent against growth's 14.35. The comparison is a statement about cash flows, because no average return entered its construction. The return columns then record the facts to explain. Value earned 13.67 percent a year against growth's 7.49, and its mean log price-dividend ratio is lower, 3.34 against 3.62. The higher premium and the lower valuation appear together. The paper's Table I, on its data vintage, prints the same pattern: 13.88 against 7.81 on returns, log P/D of 3.25 against 3.61, and volatilities of 29.9 and 20.2.

### The spread and the Sharpe ratios

::: {#cf491969 .cell execution_count=4}
``` {.python .cell-code}
wide_ret = panel.pivot(index="year", columns="claim", values="ret")
wide_dd = panel.pivot(index="year", columns="claim", values="dgrowth")
wide_pd = panel.pivot(index="year", columns="claim", values="pd")
rf_s = rf.set_index("year")["rf"]

spread = wide_ret["Value"].mean() - wide_ret["Growth"].mean()
print(f"value-growth return spread: {spread * 100:.2f} pp/yr")
sharpe_df = pd.DataFrame({
    "claim": ["Growth", "Value", "Market"],
    "sharpe": [(wide_ret[c] - rf_s).mean() / wide_ret[c].std(ddof=1)
               for c in ("Growth", "Value", "Market")],
})
(
    GT(sharpe_df)
    .tab_header(title="Sharpe ratios of the three claims")
    .cols_label(claim="", sharpe="Sharpe ratio")
    .fmt_number(columns="sharpe", decimals=2)
    .tab_options(**KIKU_GT)
)
