The spread is 6.18 points a year over seventy-four years. Value is more volatile, so the first question is whether the premium only compensates for volatility. It does not, because value's Sharpe ratio of 0.46 is higher than growth's 0.37 and the market's 0.42. Per unit of return volatility, value still pays more. (The real bill rate on our reconstruction averages near zero, at 0.07 percent as printed below, so the Sharpe ratios barely depend on it. The paper's vintage prints a mean real rate of 0.91 percent.)

